WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.402
The Reference tab of the
Federal Modernization Workbook

2
00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:06.125
holds six pages that nobody
reads front to back.

3
00:00:06.125 --> 00:00:09.511
You open them when you need an
answer: which command does this,

4
00:00:09.511 --> 00:00:13.641
which framework applies, where the
Risk Management Framework is covered,

5
00:00:13.641 --> 00:00:15.537
whether a source is still current.

6
00:00:15.537 --> 00:00:19.266
This briefing walks through each
page, the question it answers,

7
00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:22.062
and why the workbook would
be weaker without it.

8
00:00:22.062 --> 00:00:26.349
Then it shows how the pages fit together,
who should open which one first,

9
00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:29.390
and how each page tells
you how far to trust it.

10
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.790
Each book answers one question
in depth, in reading order.

11
00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.813
But an engineer, an ISSO or an
authorizing official usually

12
00:00:40.813 --> 00:00:44.753
arrives with a question that
cuts across all twenty books.

13
00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:49.277
The Reference tab exists for those
questions, and they fall into three kinds.

14
00:00:49.277 --> 00:00:53.528
Run it: which command does the
job, and which chapter teaches it.

15
00:00:53.528 --> 00:00:57.150
Prove it: which federal requirement
a piece of work satisfies,

16
00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.565
and what evidence an assessor would read.

17
00:00:59.565 --> 00:01:03.453
Trust it: whether the sources behind
all of this are still current.

18
00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:06.800
Every page on the tab
answers one of those three.

19
00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.875
Here are the six pages,
with the job each one does.

20
00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:17.150
One page helps you run the
kits: the Command Reference.

21
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.993
Four help you prove compliance: the
Federal Framework Crosswalk, the RMF Map,

22
00:01:21.993 --> 00:01:27.450
the M-19-17 and FICAM Crosswalk, and
the Microsoft Practice Crosswalk.

23
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:31.025
And one keeps everything
trustworthy: the Source Register.

24
00:01:31.025 --> 00:01:34.010
The next six slides take
them one at a time.

25
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.535
The Command Reference lists every
command in the example kits:

26
00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.922
694 of them across sixteen kits.

27
00:01:44.922 --> 00:01:49.473
That is 599 functions exported
by PowerShell modules,

28
00:01:49.473 --> 00:01:53.011
47 PowerShell scripts and 48 Python tools.

29
00:01:53.011 --> 00:01:55.335
What makes it trustworthy
is how it's made.

30
00:01:55.335 --> 00:02:00.285
The page is generated from each command's
own help text every time the site builds.

31
00:02:00.285 --> 00:02:04.780
If the help changes, the page changes
with it, so the two never drift apart,

32
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:09.123
and a public command without a synopsis
or an example fails the check.

33
00:02:09.123 --> 00:02:13.866
Each row also links back to the chapters
and training modules that teach the

34
00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:17.028
command, so an engineer can
go from what does this do,

35
00:02:17.028 --> 00:02:19.410
to where do I learn it, in one click.

36
00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.604
The Federal Framework Crosswalk
scores every book against

37
00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:30.048
the frameworks a federal
modernization program answers to.

38
00:02:30.048 --> 00:02:35.179
That goes well beyond security controls:
how IT investments are chosen and funded,

39
00:02:35.179 --> 00:02:39.783
how enterprise architecture is kept,
how digital services are delivered,

40
00:02:39.783 --> 00:02:42.273
and how privacy and records are handled.

41
00:02:42.273 --> 00:02:46.958
Each book gets a score from zero, not
addressed, to three, evidence-backed,

42
00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:51.247
for each framework, and the scores
come from reading the chapters,

43
00:02:51.247 --> 00:02:53.073
not from counting keywords.

44
00:02:53.073 --> 00:02:55.505
Just as important, the page checks each

45
00:02:55.505 --> 00:02:58.622
framework's current status
at its primary source.

46
00:02:58.622 --> 00:03:03.405
It found that OMB M-22-18
was rescinded by M-26-05,

47
00:03:03.405 --> 00:03:06.182
the Federal Data Strategy
is being retired,

48
00:03:06.182 --> 00:03:11.196
and the Federal Enterprise Architecture
Framework survives only as history,

49
00:03:11.196 --> 00:03:15.361
while Circular A-130 still requires
an enterprise architecture.

50
00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:23.110
Executive Order 14028 is in force as
amended, and FITARA has a new memo, M-26-10.

51
00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:27.147
Without that check, the books would
cite frameworks that no longer apply.

52
00:03:27.147 --> 00:03:29.120
The page is in review.

53
00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:36.340
The RMF Map is for the people
who sign off: the ISSO,

54
00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:40.070
the control assessor and
the authorizing official.

55
00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:44.038
Their question is where the Risk
Management Framework is in the workbook,

56
00:03:44.038 --> 00:03:45.795
and what each step leaves behind.

57
00:03:45.795 --> 00:03:50.570
The books answer it chapter by chapter;
this page puts the answer in one place.

58
00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:58.132
It walks all seven steps of NIST SP
800-37 Revision 2 and their 47 tasks.

59
00:03:58.132 --> 00:04:01.110
For each task it gives where
the workbook treats it,

60
00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.869
the evidence an assessor would
read, and the kit that produces it.

61
00:04:04.869 --> 00:04:08.811
The marks today: 25 covered, 13
partial, 9 partial by design,

62
00:04:08.811 --> 00:04:12.751
where the workbook gives a
template and a prompt because

63
00:04:12.751 --> 00:04:16.608
the decision belongs to the
agency, and none not covered.

64
00:04:16.608 --> 00:04:20.483
It also corrects a common
mistake: FISMA isn't a level.

65
00:04:20.483 --> 00:04:24.408
FISMA Moderate is shorthand for
a categorization and a baseline.

66
00:04:24.408 --> 00:04:29.210
The page is in review, waiting for a
reviewer who didn't write the marks.

67
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:39.560
OMB M-19-17 is the federal policy for
identity, credential and access management.

68
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:45.435
It tells agencies to run identity as one
program, to manage devices and automated

69
00:04:45.435 --> 00:04:50.697
technologies as well as people, to use
PIV and derived PIV, and to federate.

70
00:04:50.697 --> 00:04:54.648
The FICAM Architecture breaks
the same ground into services.

71
00:04:54.648 --> 00:04:59.036
This crosswalk maps both to the
workbook's books, guides and kits.

72
00:04:59.036 --> 00:05:02.599
It starts with nine identity
areas, from workforce and

73
00:05:02.599 --> 00:05:07.035
non-person identity to federation,
governance and public identity.

74
00:05:07.035 --> 00:05:12.410
Then it checks M-19-17 section by section
and the FICAM services one by one.

75
00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:17.157
Across those forty-two rows, the
marks are 15 strong, 20 partial,

76
00:05:17.157 --> 00:05:21.810
5 gaps, and 2 deliberately out of
scope, with the reason stated.

77
00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.685
Every mark comes from
reading the linked pages.

78
00:05:24.685 --> 00:05:27.835
Identity is where most of
this program's work lands,

79
00:05:27.835 --> 00:05:32.035
so naming the gaps plainly matters
as much as showing the strengths.

80
00:05:32.035 --> 00:05:34.000
The page is in review.

81
00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.550
The books recommend Microsoft
practices chapter by chapter:

82
00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:45.425
block legacy authentication,
require phishing-resistant MFA,

83
00:05:45.425 --> 00:05:49.300
put privileged roles behind
Privileged Identity Management,

84
00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.983
use device compliance
as the access signal,

85
00:05:51.983 --> 00:05:57.125
connect servers through Azure Arc, and
move SQL Server to Entra authentication.

86
00:05:57.125 --> 00:06:01.388
This crosswalk puts 33 of
those practices in one table.

87
00:06:01.388 --> 00:06:04.179
Each row gives the
practice and its chapter,

88
00:06:04.179 --> 00:06:09.219
the Microsoft Learn page that defines
it, the automated check that confirms it,

89
00:06:09.219 --> 00:06:13.173
either a CISA ScubaGear policy
ID or an Azure Policy built-in,

90
00:06:13.173 --> 00:06:15.576
and the date the row was last reviewed.

91
00:06:15.576 --> 00:06:19.650
Seeing them side by side surfaced
things no single chapter shows.

92
00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:26.450
One policy carries two version numbers
between ScubaGear and the BOD 25-01 list.

93
00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:28.912
Intune has no ScubaGear coverage.

94
00:06:28.912 --> 00:06:32.212
And Arc-enabled SQL Server
has no built-in policy

95
00:06:32.212 --> 00:06:35.512
for Entra authentication,
so it needs a custom check.

96
00:06:35.512 --> 00:06:37.330
The page is a draft.

97
00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.505
The Source Register is the
foundation under everything else.

98
00:06:44.505 --> 00:06:49.042
A slide or a video description doesn't
link straight to a publisher's page.

99
00:06:49.042 --> 00:06:53.767
It links to the source's row in this
register, and the row links to the original.

100
00:06:53.767 --> 00:06:57.405
That matters because a released
deck or video can't change,

101
00:06:57.405 --> 00:06:59.830
but publishers move pages all the time.

102
00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:01.818
With the register in between,

103
00:07:01.818 --> 00:07:06.043
a link in a released deck stays
right: only the row has to change.

104
00:07:06.043 --> 00:07:10.717
The register holds 540 sources
today, under three rules.

105
00:07:10.717 --> 00:07:16.518
A source's address is permanent: a row can
be retired, but never deleted or renamed.

106
00:07:16.518 --> 00:07:19.672
Every change goes through
a reviewed pull request,

107
00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:24.480
adds to the revision history, and sends
the page back to its owner for approval.

108
00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.705
And the weekly source watch reads
every row, shows the result in

109
00:07:28.705 --> 00:07:33.460
the Last scan column, and proposes
fixes, but never edits on its own.

110
00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.835
The pages aren't six separate
lists; they build on each other.

111
00:07:40.835 --> 00:07:44.025
The Federal Framework
Crosswalk is the wide view,

112
00:07:44.025 --> 00:07:46.360
every book against every framework.

113
00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:51.774
The RMF Map takes its Risk Management
Framework row and goes task by task; where

114
00:07:51.774 --> 00:07:57.022
the two disagree, the map wins, and the
crosswalk's RMF score is derived from it.

115
00:07:57.022 --> 00:08:02.798
The M-19-17 and FICAM Crosswalk does
the same deep dive for identity policy.

116
00:08:02.798 --> 00:08:07.626
The Microsoft Practice Crosswalk shows the
specific practices behind the evidence,

117
00:08:07.626 --> 00:08:09.394
each tied to an automated check,

118
00:08:09.394 --> 00:08:13.747
and the Command Reference lists the kit
commands that produce that evidence.

119
00:08:13.747 --> 00:08:17.798
Underneath all of them sits the
Source Register, so every source the

120
00:08:17.798 --> 00:08:22.580
slides and videos cite has a permanent
address that is checked every week.

121
00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:28.417
So who should open which page first?

122
00:08:28.417 --> 00:08:31.780
An engineer running the kits
opens the Command Reference.

123
00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.993
An ISSO or a control
assessor opens the RMF Map.

124
00:08:35.993 --> 00:08:42.280
An authorizing official reads the RMF Map,
then the M-19-17 and FICAM Crosswalk.

125
00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.868
A CIO or program lead starts with
the Federal Framework Crosswalk.

126
00:08:46.868 --> 00:08:51.654
An identity or cloud architect opens
the Microsoft Practice Crosswalk.

127
00:08:51.654 --> 00:08:55.210
And anyone citing a source
uses the Source Register.

128
00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.958
Finally, a reference page
is only useful if you

129
00:09:01.958 --> 00:09:04.798
know how far to trust it,
so each one tells you.

130
00:09:04.798 --> 00:09:08.824
The marks come from reading the
chapters, not counting keywords:

131
00:09:08.824 --> 00:09:12.623
every hit is opened in context,
and false matches are dropped.

132
00:09:12.623 --> 00:09:16.553
Each page shows its status,
draft, in review or approved,

133
00:09:16.553 --> 00:09:19.261
and the date someone last reviewed it.

134
00:09:19.261 --> 00:09:22.980
A page left unreviewed for more
than 90 days is flagged on the

135
00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:27.072
page and in the weekly source-watch
issue until someone reviews it.

136
00:09:27.072 --> 00:09:28.936
And the gaps stay on the page.

137
00:09:28.936 --> 00:09:33.237
Partial rows, gaps and out-of-scope
items are listed with their reasons,

138
00:09:33.237 --> 00:09:37.985
and where no automated check exists, the
row says so rather than inventing one.

139
00:09:37.985 --> 00:09:42.308
Every page also downloads as a Word
document, and the Reference Set

140
00:09:42.308 --> 00:09:46.710
puts all six in one file, from the
Reference tab or the Downloads page.

141
00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:47.530
Thank you.
